{
  "id": "grammar.a1.74.a",
  "title": "Six persons, six endings",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Explanation",
      "content": "You now know all six persons of the verbs from unit 13. The stem stays and the ending shows who acts. Because the ending is so clear, the pronoun is usually dropped."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Six forms of three verbs",
      "rows": [
        [
          "én (I)",
          "olvasok · beszélek · főzök"
        ],
        [
          "te (you)",
          "olvasol · beszélsz · főzöl"
        ],
        [
          "ő (he, she)",
          "olvas · beszél · főz"
        ],
        [
          "mi (we)",
          "olvasunk · beszélünk · főzünk"
        ],
        [
          "ti (you all)",
          "olvastok · beszéltek · főztök"
        ],
        [
          "ők (they)",
          "olvasnak · beszélnek · főznek"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "Examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "Ma pihenek.",
          "english": "Today I rest."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Ma pihensz?",
          "english": "Do you rest today?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Ma pihenünk.",
          "english": "Today we rest."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Ti ma pihentek?",
          "english": "Do you all rest today?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Ők ma pihennek.",
          "english": "They rest today."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Look at the end of the verb. *-sz* or *-ol/-el/-öl* is one “you”, *-tok/-tek/-tök* is “you all”, *-nak/-nek* is “they”. *Pihen* is a new verb that follows the same pattern."
    }
  ]
}
